how good are you at making decisions how  about when the clock is ticking or even  when you're feeling scared horror games  love forcing you to make decisions under  pressure and their developers love  exploiting every psychological trick in  the book to make those decisions as  difficult as possible nowhere is that  more clear than an interactive horror  titles where making choices under  pressure is the central mechanic like  telltale's The Walking Dead franchise  and supermassive games until dawn and  the Quarry ahead of the release of the  Devil in Me supermassive's latest entry  to its dark pictures Anthology series I  sat down with the game's director Tom  Heaton to break down how supermassive  approaches crafting a horror experience  where every choice you make can have  dire consequences I think with a lot of  horror games they are tied into us to  certain game modes of shooting an  exploration which you're kind of  committed to if you go down that route  but with the Cinematic room it allows  literally to explore anything that  Cinema can explore we can go there as  well so we could do extreme  psychological stuff we can do extreme  interpersonal stuff and ghosts on things  you're afraid of but can't fight those  are all up for grabs because we can  address them cinematically and we've got  a means for doing that the devil in me  follows supermassive's format of movie  style cutscenes interspersed with more  interactive exploration sections these  cutscenes are peppered with Choose Your  Own Adventure style choices some of  which are fairly mundane flirt or keep a  professional distance pay a compliment  or criticize somebody give career advice  or stay quiet and so on each of these  choices subtly diverts the plot changing  the Dynamics of relationships between  characters and potentially opening up or  locking you out of storylines much later  down the line some decisions however are  more critical run or hide fight back or  stay perfectly still if you've ever  watched a horror movie chances are  you've felt like you could make better  choices if you found yourself in these  life for death scenarios and games like  these give you the opportunity to prove  yourself right but across supermassives  games one thing sets them apart from  other horror titles the permanent  consequences of your choices their  characters often played by familiar  faces live and die by the decisions you  make no checkpoints no Second Chances  play one of our games you'll dead that  character is dead there's no reloading  of checkpoint we spend time making sure  the player understands these characters  and roots for them once we build that  that bridge that empathetic bridge to  the character and we give them a bit of  responsibility we give them a binary  Choice the situations come up less  frequently but when they come up there  they're much much more powerful and that  creates a sense of apprehension in the  player because they know in a sense  we're playing for real that death is  absolutely real and characters will  respond to it they'll react to it the  story will continue it makes those  choices come into very sharp Focus the  mechanics of how people make choices is  the subject of Decades of psychological  research analyzing the ways people try  and fail to make decisions from this  research various methods to help you  make the correct decision have been  created you've probably made a list of  pros and cons before or maybe you've  even used the decide model to help you  difficult choices but however good you  think you are at making decisions there  are two things that exert significant  psychological pressure on the  decision-making process time and emotion  it's not easy to make a list of pros and  cons with a ticking clock and as much as  we'd like to believe everyone is capable  of making rational decisions 100 of the  time as human beings we are often a  slave to our emotions particularly fear  fear affects every aspect of our  decision-making process Studies have  suggested that it affects how we make  everything from Financial to political  decisions and that it undermines our  ability to rationally assess risk  clearly within the context of a horror  game you can expect to spend a  significant part of your time with the  game feeling anxious or afraid it's no  surprise then that the most critical  decisions in these games often come at  the end of long tense sequences designed  to get you as worked up as possible  when we're toying with a player and  getting them into the game that was  scared which is what they paid for you  know they paid money to be scared of the  game that's very precious when you've  got them in that state you mustn't break  it we'll Target an area we'll Target a  bit of the game so in the Devil in Me  we've got this very creepy underground  Spa area which has got no electricity  it's all crumbling and the intention is  definitely to have the the player scared  in that area one thing that is that's  vital to do that is to have a sense of a  threat  now a threat might be real or it might  not be it might be imaginary but you  absolutely need it it's very difficult  to scare people at the beach in broad  daylight because there's very little  threat but if you take those people out  into a forest at night then they will  start to perceive a threat their  imaginations work kind of an overdrive  and that's what we want as developers we  want the players imaginations to be  working in overdrive so they so they  have to perceive a threat and in the  horror game the threat is usually fairly  well defined it's a killer or it's a  vampire you know you know it's there so  now you're in this place where there's a  potential threat and then I think the  next thing we do is we create  uncertainty and ambiguity you hear that  if by that you mean the terrifying sound  then no I didn't hear anything door  closing something falling off a shelf  you don't know if you need to associate  that noise with a threat or not so we  we're raising the stakes all the time  we're raising your attention level we're  really trying to trigger something very  Primal but trying to get your fight or  flight response going  like we have a choice on our hands  fight or flight or fight flight freeze  response is a hardwired reaction to  threatening situations that prepares the  body to survive through the stimulation  of our sympathetic nervous system though  it causes several obvious physiological  changes in the body like an increased  heart rate and increased blood flow to  the brain and muscles it has  psychological effects too quickening  your speed of thought and focusing your  attention on perceived threats  controlling your reactions to  fight-or-flight situations is known as  emotional self-regulation and Studies  have suggested effective emotion  regulation can help you make more  rational decisions when faced with  anxiety-inducing situations but in the  context of a horror game where it feels  like there are no good decisions that  may be easier said than done the whole  point about a fight or flight is that  both of those are probably quite good  options and doing nothing is is usually  the worst option you know so if you're  playing a shooter for example shooting  is usually a good outcome of flight is a  last resort but we try and balance that  up very consciously and sometimes with  really you know a really straightforward  choices to the to the player do you want  to run or hide or do nothing do I just  ride this out and see how it goes we're  putting players into a dilemma but  that's really the very end point of the  the first day I think we've got you into  that bear state to to make the that  decision more difficult get those  decisions wrong and you may come face to  face with another staple of  supermassives games a Grizzly death for  one of your favorite characters Goodbye  cruel world the final curtain is calling  and there's no time for an encore  [Music]  though the devil in me's masked  antagonist is inspired by real-life  serial killer H.H Holmes in the world of  the game supermassive also Drew  inspiration from torture horror movies  like the saw franchise creating what  they say is set to be their glorious  game to date I hate looking at court  because then I start to feel seeing it  again so it's it's kind of that in a way  becomes  threat I'm I'm worried about this how  can I avoid this isn't going to spring  on me suddenly the court really triggers  fight or flight very very aggressively  again you mustn't overuse it it's like  we're cooking the player in this first  day we're just getting them on a rolling  boil with fear we mustn't tend to high  because then they'll they'll have a big  point and then they have to have a come  down but we mustn't let them get out of  it we try to sustain it for as long as  possible and I think all contributors to  that is all things the player is worried  about seeing they're very effective for  them  if you're not a horror fan being  confronted with these gory deaths  stress-inducing situations and Razor's  Edge decisions may not sound like a  great idea but as we've covered  previously on Mind Games some studies  suggest that experiencing recreational  fear through horror media has benefits  for developing your skills in emotional  regulation within the context of these  games in particular this makes a lot of  sense it feels like having the  experience of making decisions under the  kinds of pressure these games exert on  us is a very good opportunity to  rehearse your responses to stressful  situations and the choice-based  mechanics make for a much more natural  transition to reality than games which  involve more complex systems of shooting  survival or Resource Management Maybe by  playing these games you're hoping to  train yourself to make better Karma  decisions Under Pressure let's just hope  none of us are ever faced with these  kinds of decisions  I read a great article which said horror  was like self-administering trauma  therapy and I think that's I think  that's true it's about being able to go  to a space and entertain ideas that are  not comfortable I think especially so  for games not only do you experience it  vicariously this horrible idea but you  experience it and you have the  opportunity to see how you would respond  to it horror is about trauma it's about  experiencing things that you're really  really scared of  um and and kind of practicing about how  you would cope with them so I think  that's in in general that's a good thing
 

